    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "Simplify $\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{5}-2}+\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{5}+2}$ by forming the lowest common denominator.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$5\\sqrt{5}+2$",
      "worked": "The lowest common denominator is $(\\sqrt{5}-2)(\\sqrt{5}+2)=5-4=1$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}-2}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}+2}&=\\frac{3(\\sqrt{5}+2)+2(\\sqrt{5}-2)}{(\\sqrt{5}-2)(\\sqrt{5}+2)}\\\\\n\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}-2}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}+2}&=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}+6+2\\sqrt{5}-4}{5-4}\\\\\n\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}-2}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}+2}&=5\\sqrt{5}+2.\n\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
